{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-129344",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}-4}{16-2(7)+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-4}{16-14-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-y}-4}{16-2x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-y}-4)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{2x-y-16}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{16-2x+y}{(16-2x+y)(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{\\cancel{16-2x+y}}{\\cancel{(16-2x+y)}(\\sqrt{2x-y}+4)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{lim}_{x\\to (7,-2)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-y}+4} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2(7)-(-2)}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{8}.$",
